{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Move fsck's shared filesystem primitives out of `fsck_repair.py`\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nSurfaced by the architectural analysis on `defaceable-icy-antelope` (which split\n`_commands/fsck.py` into a command driver, `fsck_scan.py` and `fsck_tracker_health.py`).\n\nThree pure filesystem primitives live in `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py`:\n\n- `_resolve_tracker_git_dir` — resolve the tracker's git dir (handles the worktree pointer file);\n- `_ticket_dirs` — the shared store-walk iterator (sorted ticket dirs, active-only by default);\n- `_dir_is_archived` — the archival predicate `_ticket_dirs` filters with, and `fsck_scan`'s own\n  import.\n\nThey are there for a historical reason, not a structural one: `fsck_repair` was the first module\nextracted out of `fsck.py`, and the helpers went along with the cluster that happened to move\nfirst.\n\nThe result is an inverted dependency. None of the three has anything to do with repair, yet\n**seven** production modules now reach into the **repair** module to get them — four via `fsck`\nor a direct module-level import, and three via a deferred function-local import:\n\n| consumer | symbols | how |\n|---|---|---|\n| `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_scan.py` | `_dir_is_archived`, `_ticket_dirs`, `_resolve_tracker_git_dir` | module-level, from `fsck_repair` |\n| `src/rebar/_commands/fsck.py` | `_ticket_dirs`, `_resolve_tracker_git_dir` | module-level re-export, from `fsck_repair` |\n| `src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py` | `_resolve_tracker_git_dir` | **two** function-local imports, from `fsck` |\n| `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_recover.py` | `_resolve_tracker_git_dir` | module-level, from `fsck` |\n| `src/rebar/_commands/compact.py` | `_ticket_dirs` | function-local, from `fsck_repair` |\n| `src/rebar/_commands/bridge_repair.py` | `_resolve_tracker_git_dir` | function-local, from `fsck_repair` |\n| `src/rebar/_commands/tracker_maintenance.py` | `_resolve_tracker_git_dir` | function-local, from `fsck_repair` |\n\nSo a store-layer module (`gitutil`) depends on a command-layer *repair* module to answer 'where\nis the tracker's git dir', and the diagnostic path cannot be read without loading the mutating\none. All three primitives are also the sort of thing a future non-fsck caller would want, and\nwould have no reason to look for under 'repair'.\n\n## Approach\n\nMove all three primitives to `src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py` and repoint every consumer.\n\n**Why `gitutil`, and the circular-import check the move is conditioned on.** `gitutil` is a\ndeclared stdlib-only leaf: its module level imports `fcntl`/`logging`/`os`/`random`/`re`/\n`subprocess`/`time` and **no** `rebar.*` module at all. Each moved primitive keeps that property:\n`_resolve_tracker_git_dir` and `_ticket_dirs` need only `os`, and `_dir_is_archived`'s\n`rebar.reducer._api._is_net_archived` import is already **function-local** and stays that way.\nEvery consumer is a `_commands` module importing `_store`, which is the direction that already\nexists (`fsck_repair` imports `run_git` from `gitutil` at module level today). So the graph is\nacyclic and the ticket's `gitutil` default holds; a new `_store` leaf is not needed, and would\nalso land under the \"never create files < 100 LOC by splitting\" rule in AGENTS.md. AC6 checks\nthis mechanically rather than by assertion.\n\n**Module size.** `gitutil` 614 -> ~665 LOC, `fsck_repair` 686 -> ~640 LOC; both stay under the\n800-LOC hard cap.\n\n**Compatibility surfaces that must survive — two, not one.**\n\n1. `fsck.py` keeps re-exporting `_ticket_dirs` and `_resolve_tracker_git_dir` (now imported from\n   `gitutil`), so `fsck.<symbol>` attribute access keeps resolving. That surface is documented in\n   the architectural-analysis comment on `defaceable-icy-antelope`, and is bound by\n   `tests/interfaces/store/test_event_append_transient_retry.py`,\n   `tests/interfaces/store/test_stale_lock_reclaim_write.py`,\n   `tests/interfaces/store/test_fsck_index_lock_reclaim_toctou.py` and\n   `tests/integration/test_concurrency_regression.py`.\n2. `fsck_repair.py` keeps **module-level bindings** for all three names (re-imported from\n   `gitutil`). This is load-bearing and easy to miss: three tests bind to the names as\n   *module attributes on `fsck_repair`*, which the LSP cannot see —\n   `monkeypatch.setattr(fsck_repair, \"_ticket_dirs\", ...)` and\n   `monkeypatch.setattr(fsck_repair, \"_resolve_tracker_git_dir\", ...)` in\n   `tests/scripts/graph/test_repair_pause_ownership.py` and\n   `tests/interfaces/store/test_repair_pause_ownership_heldout.py`, and\n   `_fsck_repair._ticket_dirs(...)` in `tests/unit/test_scan_scoping.py`. Keeping the names as\n   `fsck_repair` module globals means those patches still take effect on `fsck_repair`'s own\n   call sites (which resolve through the module global) and no test file needs editing (AC7).\n\nThe three function-local importers (`compact`, `bridge_repair`, `tracker_maintenance`) are\nrepointed at `gitutil` rather than left riding `fsck_repair`'s compat re-import — leaving them\nwould preserve the very inverted dependency this ticket removes.\n\nBehaviour-preserving: no logic change, function bodies moved verbatim, imports repointed only.\n\n## Testing\n\nNo new behaviour, so the existing suite is the oracle and is run **unmodified** (AC7). The\nexecutable RED oracle for the move itself is the AC grep set: AC1/AC2/AC3/AC6 all fail on\n`origin/main` and pass after the move, and that before/after run is recorded as a ticket comment.\n\nThe bindings at risk are exercised by `tests/interfaces/store/test_event_append_transient_retry.py`\nand `tests/interfaces/store/test_stale_lock_reclaim_write.py` (both import\n`_resolve_tracker_git_dir` from `fsck`), by `tests/scripts/graph/test_repair_pause_ownership.py`,\n`tests/interfaces/store/test_repair_pause_ownership_heldout.py` and\n`tests/unit/test_scan_scoping.py` (all three bind to `fsck_repair` module attributes), and by the\nfsck suites under `tests/interfaces/store/`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] AC1: none of the three primitives is defined in `src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py` —\n      verified by\n      `! grep -qE '^def (_ticket_dirs|_resolve_tracker_git_dir|_dir_is_archived)' src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py`\n      exiting 0.\n- [ ] AC2: all three are defined in `src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py` — verified by\n      `test \"$(grep -cE '^def (_ticket_dirs|_resolve_tracker_git_dir|_dir_is_archived)' src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py)\" = 3`\n      exiting 0.\n- [ ] AC3: no module under `src/rebar/` imports any of the three from `fsck` or `fsck_repair` —\n      verified by\n      `! grep -rqE 'from rebar\\._commands\\.fsck(_repair)? import.*(_ticket_dirs|_resolve_tracker_git_dir|_dir_is_archived)' src/rebar/`\n      exiting 0. (This covers the seven consumers, including the three function-local importers\n      `compact.py`, `bridge_repair.py` and `tracker_maintenance.py`.)\n- [ ] AC4: the `fsck` re-export surface still resolves — verified by\n      `python -c \"from rebar._commands import fsck; fsck._resolve_tracker_git_dir; fsck._ticket_dirs\"`\n      exiting 0.\n- [ ] AC5: the `fsck_repair` module-attribute surface the un-editable tests patch still resolves —\n      verified by\n      `python -c \"from rebar._commands import fsck_repair; fsck_repair._resolve_tracker_git_dir; fsck_repair._ticket_dirs; fsck_repair._dir_is_archived\"`\n      exiting 0.\n- [ ] AC6: the new home introduced no cycle and `gitutil` is still a stdlib-only leaf — verified by\n      `python -c \"import rebar._store.gitutil\"` exiting 0 AND\n      `! grep -qE '^(from|import) rebar' src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py` exiting 0.\n- [ ] AC7: no test file is edited — verified by `git diff --stat origin/main -- tests/` producing\n      no output.\n- [ ] AC8: `make lint` exits 0.\n- [ ] AC9: `make typecheck` exits 0.\n- [ ] AC10: `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` exits 0 (no `new`/`increased`).\n- [ ] AC11: `pytest -m \"not integration and not external\"` exits 0.\n- [ ] AC12: every `src/rebar` file stays under the 800-LOC cap — verified by the CI module-size\n      gate; locally `wc -l src/rebar/_store/gitutil.py src/rebar/_commands/fsck_repair.py` both\n      report < 800."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787175288611993001,"uuid":"49343e81-acf5-4162-83ad-b294dfa39d09"}